    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_chordal - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_chordal.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_chordal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if an undirected graph is chordal (triangulated).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_chordal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if an undirected graph is chordal (triangulated).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_chordal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if an undirected graph is chordal (triangulated).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if an undirected graph is chordal (triangulated).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33632-L33723){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_chordal()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is chordal](../assets/graphs/is-chordal.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a square A, B, C, D with the diagonal A to C, which splits it into two triangles, so every cycle of four or more nodes has a chord and is_chordal() returns True. Right: the square without the diagonal; the cycle A, B, C, D (shown in the accent color) has no chord, so is_chordal() returns False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA graph is chordal when every cycle of four or more nodes has a *chord*, an edge joining\ntwo nodes of the cycle that are not consecutive on it; equivalently, it has no induced\ncycle longer than a triangle. Trees, complete graphs, interval graphs and k-trees are\nall chordal. Chordal graphs are where many hard problems become easy: maximum cliques,\noptimal colorings and tree decompositions can all be read off a perfect elimination\nordering, which is why sparse matrix factorization, Bayesian network inference\n(junction trees) and phylogenetics rely on them.\n\nParallel edges count once and self-loops are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if the graph is chordal. Graphs with at most three nodes always are.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nMaximum cardinality search (Tarjan and Yannakakis, 1984) numbers the nodes one at a\ntime, always picking the node with the most numbered neighbors; the graph is chordal\nexactly when, for every node, its previously numbered neighbors form a clique, in which\ncase the reverse order is a perfect elimination ordering. Results match NetworkX's\n`is_chordal`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~complete_to_chordal_graph`) \n\n:   Add edges to make a graph chordal.\n\n[](`~chordal_graph_cliques`) \n\n:   List the maximal cliques of a chordal graph.\n\n[](`~is_perfect_graph`) \n\n:   Check if every induced subgraph colors with its largest clique.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA square has a cycle of four with no chord, so it isn't chordal. A diagonal cuts it into\ntwo triangles:\n\n::: {#7f5d49d0 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nsquare = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"A\")]\n)\nbefore = square.is_chordal()\nsquare.add_edge(\"A\", \"C\")\n\nbefore, square.is_chordal()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(False, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Ready for a junction tree\n\n`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each\ncause to its effect. Its moral graph, which joins the parents of each variable and drops\nthe directions, is chordal, so a junction tree can be built from it without adding edges.\n`exams()`, which joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student takes both,\nisn't:\n\n::: {#92314ade .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmoral = ec.causal().moral_graph()\nexams = ec.exams()\n\nmoral.is_chordal(), exams.is_chordal()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn `exams()`, Philosophy, Literature, Economics, History and Biology form a cycle of five\ncourses with no chord:\n\n::: {#b8d1ca42 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nhole = [\"Philosophy\", \"Literature\", \"Economics\", \"History\", \"Biology\"]\nexams.plot(\n    highlight=[*hole, hole[0]], label_by=\"label\", seed=3, height=420\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-87